        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "The UKâ€™s incoming prime minister Andy Burnham has remained tight-lipped on his views on climate change during his leadership campaign. When asked his views on reversing Labourâ€™s manifesto pledge to stop new North Sea drilling in June â€“ a move that the oil-and-gas industry and right-wing media have pushed for in recent months â€“ he said he had â€œsomething of an open mindâ€ on the issue. But a trawl of Burnhamâ€™s past comments about climate change, net-zero and fossil fuels reveals a different picture. Just a year ago in June 2025, Burnham, while mayor of Greater Manchester, gave his support to the fossil fuel treaty â€“ a proposed international pact on phasing out coal, oil and gas â€“ calling it a â€œlifelineâ€ that â€œall governmentsâ€ should join. In a video message endorsing the treaty, he also said that â€œthere should be no turning away from net-zeroâ€. During his last bid to be Labour leader in 2015, he used similar language, saying:&nbsp; â€œLabour under my leadership will never turn our back on either our duty to tackle climate change or the prospects offered by the green economy.â€ Burnham has spoken about the threat of climate change since at least 2008, noting in 2021 that accelerated action could â€œcreate thousands of good jobsâ€, but also warning that net-zero risked becoming the â€œnext Brexitâ€. Burnham is yet to appoint his cabinet, but there is much speculation that he will select current net-zero secretary Ed Miliband as his chancellor â€“ with their ally Miatta Fahnbulleh having a â€œ strong chance â€ of taking Milibandâ€™s former position. Below, Carbon Brief recounts 28 things that Burnham has said about climate change, net-zero, fossil fuels, energy and transport. Climate change&nbsp; â€œTackling climate chanƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2fsD14MtCj8p1IK1Pcm03Vpia-I--unKa-cbaXnb9A4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcurren